 "answer": "Among Iowa's four largest food banks (by FY2023 total revenue), **Northeast Iowa Food Bank** (Waterloo, EIN 42-1169648) relies most heavily on donations \u2014 contributions and grants made up **97.3%** of its FY2023 (fiscal year ended June 2023) total revenue of $21.0 million.\n\nFor comparison, contributions' share of total revenue in FY2023 (all fiscal years ending mid-2023) was:\n\n| Food bank | Total revenue (FY2023) | Contributions share |\n|---|---|---|\n| Northeast Iowa Food Bank, Waterloo | $20.99M | **97.3%** |\n| Food Bank of Iowa, Des Moines | $47.20M | 96.0% |\n| Food Bank of Siouxland, Sioux City (FYE Sept 2023) | $6.75M | 95.2% |\n| River Bend Food Reservoir, Davenport | $39.14M | 93.0% |\n\nMethodology/caveats:\n- I searched \"food bank,\" \"foodbank,\" and \"food reservoir\" for Iowa and used the four organizations with the largest total revenue as \"largest food banks\"; this list may not be exhaustive (e.g., it excludes the small Food Bank of Southern Iowa, which last filed in 2017 with only ~$480K revenue, and Linn Community Food Bank, which despite its name reports well under $1M in revenue and likely isn't a primary regional food bank).\n- \"Depends on donations\" is measured here as contributions & grants \u00f7 total revenue, per each org's most recent Form 990 (tax year 2023, several with fiscal years ending June 2023).",
